---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import Geometry3DSim from '../../simulators/geometry3d/Geometry3DSim.astro';
import { geometry3dQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/geometry3d';

const toc = [
  { id: 'solids', label: 'Solid shapes' },
  { id: 'parts', label: 'Faces, edges and vertices' },
  { id: 'volume', label: 'Volume' },
  { id: 'area', label: 'Surface area' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formula sheet' },
  { id: 'sections', label: 'Cross-sections' },
  { id: 'scaling', label: 'Scaling and the square–cube law' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Abramson, J. (2021). Prealgebra 2e, Section 9.6: Solve Geometry Applications: Volume and Surface Area. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/9-6-solve-geometry-applications-volume-and-surface-area' },
  { text: 'Richeson, D. S. (2008). Euler’s Gem: The Polyhedron Formula and the Birth of Topology. Princeton University Press.' },
  { text: 'Coxeter, H. S. M. (1973). Regular Polytopes (3rd ed.). Dover.' },
  { text: 'Haldane, J. B. S. (1926). On Being the Right Size. Harper’s Magazine.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="mathematics"
  topic="geometry-3d"
  description="Interactive 3D geometry explorer: rotate and resize cubes, prisms, pyramids, cylinders, cones and spheres, read volume and surface area, check Euler’s formula and slice shapes into cross-sections."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={geometry3dQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Boxes, cans, ice-cream cones and footballs are all solid shapes with exact rules for how much they hold and how
      much material covers them. Pick a shape, spin it round, change its size and slice it open to see the geometry
      that connects them all.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Geometry3DSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>shape</strong> from the list. <strong>Drag</strong> to rotate it; scroll or pinch to zoom (tap first on touch screens).</li>
      <li>Use the sliders to change its <strong>dimensions</strong> and watch the <strong>volume</strong> and <strong>surface area</strong> update. The grid squares are 1 cm.</li>
      <li>Check <strong>Faces, edges, vertices</strong> for every flat-faced shape: does F − E + V always equal 2?</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Slice the shape</strong> and move the <strong>slice height</strong> to see the cross-section (orange) and its area.</li>
      <li>Double a length and see how much the volume and area grow.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="solids">Solid shapes</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>three-dimensional (3D)</strong> shape has length, width and height. Shapes with only flat faces and straight
    edges are called <strong>polyhedra</strong>: cubes, cuboids, prisms and pyramids. Shapes with curved surfaces, such as
    cylinders, cones and spheres, are not polyhedra. A <strong>prism</strong> has the same cross-section all along its
    length, while a <strong>pyramid</strong> or cone narrows to a point called the <strong>apex</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parts">Faces, edges and vertices</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>face</strong> is a flat surface, an <strong>edge</strong> is where two faces meet, and a
    <strong>vertex</strong> (plural vertices) is a corner. In 1750 Leonhard Euler noticed that for every convex
    polyhedron:
  </p>
  <p><code class="formula">F − E + V = 2</code></p>
  <p>
    A cube has 6 − 12 + 8 = 2; a tetrahedron 4 − 6 + 4 = 2. This simple rule was one of the first results in
    <strong>topology</strong>, the mathematics of shape.
  </p>

  <h2 id="volume">Volume</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Volume</strong> is the amount of space a shape fills, measured in cubic units such as cm³. For any prism
    (including cuboids and cylinders), volume = area of cross-section × length. A pyramid or cone holds exactly one third
    of the prism or cylinder with the same base and height, which you can show by filling one with sand or water three
    times. Archimedes proved that a sphere has two thirds of the volume of the cylinder that just encloses it, and was so
    proud of it that he asked for the diagram on his tomb.
  </p>

  <h2 id="area">Surface area</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Surface area</strong> is the total area of all the outside surfaces, in square units such as cm². For
    polyhedra, add up the areas of all the faces; it helps to imagine unfolding the shape into a flat
    <strong>net</strong>. The curved surface of a cylinder unrolls into a rectangle whose length is the circumference,
    2πr, giving 2πrh. A cone’s curved surface is a sector of a circle with area πrl, where l is the slant height.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formula sheet</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Volume</th><th>Surface area</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cube (side a)</td><td>a³</td><td>6a²</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cuboid</td><td>l × w × h</td><td>2(lw + lh + wh)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cylinder</td><td>πr²h</td><td>2πr² + 2πrh</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cone</td><td>⅓πr²h</td><td>πr² + πrl</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sphere</td><td>⁴⁄₃πr³</td><td>4πr²</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Square pyramid</td><td>⅓a²h</td><td>a² + 2as</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sections">Cross-sections</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>cross-section</strong> is the flat shape you get when you slice through a solid. Slices of a prism parallel
    to its end are all identical. Slices of a pyramid or cone parallel to the base are the same shape as the base but
    shrink towards the apex: halfway up, the side length is halved and the area is a quarter. Slices of a sphere are
    always circles, largest through the centre. Cross-sections are used in medicine (CT scans), engineering and
    3D printing, which builds objects one slice at a time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scaling">Scaling and the square–cube law</h2>
  <p>
    If every length of a shape is multiplied by a scale factor k, its surface area is multiplied by k² and its volume by
    k³. Double the size and you get 4 times the area but 8 times the volume. This <strong>square–cube law</strong> explains
    why small animals lose heat quickly, why cells divide rather than growing huge, and why giant insects from films could
    not exist: their legs could not support their weight.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Polyhedron</dt>
    <dd>A solid with flat polygon faces, straight edges and vertices.</dd>
    <dt>Net</dt>
    <dd>A flat pattern that folds up to make a 3D shape.</dd>
    <dt>Slant height (l or s)</dt>
    <dd>The distance from the apex to the edge of the base, measured along the surface.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Doubling the sides doubles the volume.”</strong> It multiplies the volume by 8.</li>
    <li><strong>“A cylinder has no edges.”</strong> It has two curved edges where the flat faces meet the curved surface.</li>
    <li><strong>“Surface area and volume use the same units.”</strong> Area is in square units (cm²), volume in cubic units (cm³).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Volume measures space inside (cm³); surface area measures the outside covering (cm²).</li>
    <li>Prisms: V = cross-section × length. Pyramids and cones: one third of the matching prism or cylinder.</li>
    <li>For polyhedra, F − E + V = 2.</li>
    <li>Scaling lengths by k scales area by k² and volume by k³.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: curved shapes are drawn with many small flat panels, but the readouts use the exact formulas.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
